{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>They are more than the hairs of my head</b> — those who destroy me. This is according to their [the poets'] way of thinking, and similarly: \"And Balak son of Zippor king of Moab saw, and waged war against Israel\" — [where] the men pursued after them [after Moab, understood as hostile foes]."], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 69, :notes {0 "Ibn Ezra cites Numbers 22:2–3; the argument appears to be that 'seeing' (ראה) can imply hostile intent or that a verb's subject can be read inversely, though the compressed logic is not fully spelled out."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:33:22.906170455Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}